> **3c. V16 — WHAT EXPEDITING COSTS, WHAT IS PRESERVED, HOW THE ORDER STAYS VISIBLE.** COST: (i) the NIST primary is, in practice, given up — under V15 NIST had 24 h to serve a verifying certificate and would then have been binding; under V16 it has the ten minutes between approval and T_pulse, so the seed will almost certainly come from drand, whose BLS proof this lane cannot verify (LIMIT (ii)); the seed's provenance therefore rests on agreement of ≥ 2 of 4 pinned relays, retained and live, not on a signature verified to a pinned root — a weaker provenance, accepted with eyes open; (ii) the V15 signature and its RETRY record become history: one signed commitment whose seed was never drawn, on file forever, and the register will show a V15 signed-then-superseded row; (iii) nothing else — no sample size, floor, bar, exclusion, attempt count, custody or protection rule moves. PRESERVED: V15's bytes and digest, its signature record, the RETRY beacon record, every pin V15 carries except the three files named above. ORDER: MIN_T_SIGN and EXCLUDED_T_PULSE (step (1a)) make it impossible for a V16 record to name a pulse that existed when the expedite was requested (11:05 KST = 02:05Z) or approved; the change record carries the timeline (finding 11:01, dispatch 11:06, confirmation 11:08, code 11:12–11:14, this text 11:16, gate, approval, T_pulse) so a reader sees request → authorisation → approval → pulse in that order.
